1
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:05,680
Twenty-eight thousand years ago, a lion cub died in Siberia.

2
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:07,720
It was barely a month old.

3
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:10,900
The ground froze around it, and it stayed frozen.

4
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:14,940
In 2018, it came out of the riverbank still covered in fur.

5
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:18,670
Its paws, teeth and even its whiskers were intact.

6
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,910
Scientists named it Sparta.

7
00:00:20,960 --> 00:00:22,980
Sparta was a cave lion.

8
00:00:23,030 --> 00:00:24,900
If it had lived, it would have grown into one

9
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:27,800
of the largest cats that ever walked the Earth.

10
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:30,790
And it shared the Ice Age with something even stranger.

11
00:00:30,840 --> 00:00:35,000
Human beings. Our ancestors lived alongside cave lions,

12
00:00:35,050 --> 00:00:37,110
saber-toothed cats, giant bears and

13
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:40,090
wolves heavier than any alive today.

14
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:42,650
They didn't watch these animals from a safe distance.

15
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:46,620
They hunted where these predators hunted and slept where they prowled.

16
00:00:46,670 --> 00:00:49,740
So here's the question. If you were dropped into the Ice Age with

17
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:54,350
nothing but your bare hands, which of these predators could you actually beat?

18
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,520
Let's start with the one that seems the most beatable.

19
00:00:56,570 --> 00:00:59,920
And work our way up to the ones that weren't even close.

20
00:00:59,970 --> 00:01:03,960
In the middle of Los Angeles, there's a place where the ground is still sticky.

21
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:07,600
The La Brea Tar Pits. For tens of thousands of years,

22
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:10,460
natural asphalt seeped up through the soil there.

23
00:01:10,510 --> 00:01:12,950
Animals walked into it and got stuck.

24
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,440
Then predators came to eat the trapped animals,

25
00:01:15,490 --> 00:01:18,510
and they got stuck too. When scientists started digging,

26
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:20,510
they expected a mix of animals.

27
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:23,500
What they found was a wall of predators.

28
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:27,400
The most common large mammal at La Brea isn't a bison or a horse.

29
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:30,150
It's the dire wolf. Researchers have recovered the

30
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:32,250
remains of thousands of them.

31
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:36,530
At first, dire wolves were thought to be a kind of oversized gray wolf.

32
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:39,230
That turned out to be wrong. In 2021, scientists analyzed

33
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:42,840
ancient DNA from dire wolf bones.

34
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,280
The results surprised everyone. Dire wolves weren't close

35
00:01:46,330 --> 00:01:48,230
relatives of gray wolves at all.

36
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:50,320
Their lineage split off millions of years

37
00:01:50,370 --> 00:01:53,070
ago and evolved on its own in the Americas.

38
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:55,560
Physically, a dire wolf was similar in size to a

39
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:59,080
large gray wolf, but heavier and more solidly built.

40
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:01,630
It probably weighed around sixty kilograms.

41
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:05,720
Its skull was broad, and its teeth were built for crushing bone.

42
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:09,775
So could you beat one? Honestly, even this is a bad matchup.

43
00:02:09,825 --> 00:02:11,800
A sixty-kilogram animal with a

44
00:02:11,850 --> 00:02:13,900
bone-crushing bite would be faster than you,

45
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:16,390
and it would have a weapon you don't.

46
00:02:16,440 --> 00:02:18,410
Your best hope would be to protect your throat with

47
00:02:18,460 --> 00:02:21,490
your arm and fight back with everything you have.

48
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:23,940
Some people have survived wolf attacks this way.

49
00:02:23,990 --> 00:02:26,760
It's possible. But there's a much bigger problem.

50
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:29,780
Dire wolves almost certainly lived in packs.

51
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:33,470
You wouldn't be fighting one wolf. You'd be fighting all of them.

52
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:36,800
And they're not even the most dangerous animal from that tar pit.

53
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:38,770
Across Ice Age Europe and Asia,

54
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:41,390
another predator was cracking bones.

55
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:43,810
In 1821, workers at a quarry in Yorkshire,

56
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:47,130
England, broke into a cave called Kirkdale.

57
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,840
Inside, the floor was covered in bones.

58
00:02:49,890 --> 00:02:53,110
There were elephants, rhinos and hippos.

59
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,810
Bones from animals no one expected to find in England.

60
00:02:56,860 --> 00:03:00,470
Many of the bones were gnawed. Some were split open.

61
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:03,210
The geologist William Buckland investigated.

62
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:05,380
He noticed the bite marks and the teeth mixed in

63
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:08,480
with the remains, and he realized what had happened.

64
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:11,080
This cave was a hyena den.

65
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:13,090
For generations, hyenas had dragged

66
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:18,140
their prey inside and eaten it there.

67
00:03:18,190 --> 00:03:19,500
These were cave hyenas. They were closely related to today's

68
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:22,450
spotted hyenas, but larger and heavier.

69
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:24,880
And spotted hyenas today are already among the

70
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:27,260
most powerful biters in the animal world,

71
00:03:27,310 --> 00:03:30,750
capable of cracking bones that a lion leaves behind.

72
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:33,080
So could you beat a cave hyena?

73
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:36,870
Probably not. A heavier hyena with jaws that could shatter a rhino

74
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:39,720
bone wouldn't need many bites to end the fight.

75
00:03:39,770 --> 00:03:42,780
And like the dire wolf, hyenas were social.

76
00:03:42,830 --> 00:03:44,830
Spotted hyenas today live in clans

77
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:49,050
that can number in the dozens. But these hunters relied on numbers.

78
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:51,640
The next predator didn't need them.

79
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:53,670
Back at La Brea, the second most common

80
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,300
predator is the one everyone recognizes.

81
00:03:56,350 --> 00:03:58,750
Smilodon. The saber-toothed cat.

82
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,040
Its upper canines were longer than a human hand.

83
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:04,090
Its body was heavily muscled,

84
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:06,040
especially in the front legs and neck.

85
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:09,390
The California species, Smilodon fatalis,

86
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:12,930
likely weighed somewhere around two hundred kilograms or more.

87
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:16,130
In South America, its relative, Smilodon populator,

88
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:19,800
may have reached around four hundred. Here's the strange part.

89
00:04:19,850 --> 00:04:25,140
Despite those enormous teeth, Smilodon had a fairly weak bite for a cat its size.

90
00:04:25,190 --> 00:04:28,430
So how did it kill? Scientists think it used its powerful

91
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:31,040
forelimbs to pin prey down first.

92
00:04:31,090 --> 00:04:33,310
Only then, once the animal couldn't struggle,

93
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,610
did it drive its long canines into the throat.

94
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:39,240
The teeth were deadly, but also fragile.

95
00:04:39,290 --> 00:04:41,310
They could break if the prey thrashed too hard.

96
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:43,440
That sounds like a weakness.

97
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:47,480
And in a way, it is. But for you, it doesn't help much.

98
00:04:47,530 --> 00:04:49,500
Smilodon's arms alone were strong

99
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:51,660
enough to hold down horses and bison.

100
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:54,770
A human would be pinned in seconds.

101
00:04:54,820 --> 00:04:58,470
And there's another detail that makes Smilodon even more unsettling.

102
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:02,210
Some skeletons from La Brea show severe injuries that had healed.

103
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:05,630
Damaged hips. Broken bones that would have made hunting

104
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:08,370
nearly impossible for a long time.

105
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:10,300
Yet these cats survived.

106
00:05:10,350 --> 00:05:12,140
Some researchers think that means other

107
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:15,230
Smilodon helped them, perhaps by sharing food.

108
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:19,010
Others argue a wounded cat could have survived by scavenging alone.

109
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:22,630
The debate isn't settled. But if Smilodon lived in groups,

110
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:24,690
you might never meet just one.

111
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:27,320
And Smilodon wasn't the only saber-tooth.

112
00:05:27,370 --> 00:05:30,580
In 2020, near a river in Yakutia, in far eastern Siberia,

113
00:05:30,630 --> 00:05:34,510
a frozen animal emerged from the permafrost.

114
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:37,090
It was small. Covered in dark fur.

115
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:40,640
Its face was preserved so well it almost looked asleep.

116
00:05:40,690 --> 00:05:43,720
At first glance, it looked like it could be a lion cub.

117
00:05:43,770 --> 00:05:46,270
But when scientists studied it, they realized it was

118
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:48,990
something no one had ever seen in the flesh before.

119
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,570
It was a Homotherium cub.

120
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,830
Homotherium was a scimitar-toothed cat.

121
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:56,780
Its teeth were shorter and broader than Smilodon's,

122
00:05:56,830 --> 00:05:58,680
and its body was built differently.

123
00:05:58,730 --> 00:06:01,240
It had long front legs and a sloping back,

124
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:04,480
more like a hyena's. The researchers estimated that the cub

125
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:06,540
was about three weeks old when it died,

126
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:09,180
more than thirty-five thousand years ago.

127
00:06:09,230 --> 00:06:13,460
For more than a century, scientists only knew Homotherium from bones.

128
00:06:13,510 --> 00:06:17,990
Now they could see its fur, its paws and even the shape of its mouth.

129
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:20,080
The cub's neck was thick and powerful.

130
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:22,640
Its front legs were already sturdy.

131
00:06:22,690 --> 00:06:24,850
Even at three weeks old, its body showed

132
00:06:24,900 --> 00:06:27,370
the kind of predator it was going to become.

133
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,600
Researchers think adult Homotherium may have

134
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:32,890
hunted in groups, possibly going after large prey.

135
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,170
Some scientists have even suggested it took on

136
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:38,210
young mammoths, though that idea is still debated.

137
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:40,130
Could you beat one? No.

138
00:06:40,180 --> 00:06:42,940
Not as a pack, and almost certainly not alone either.

139
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:45,590
A cat built to bring down prey many times

140
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:48,130
your size wouldn't need to try very hard.

141
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:50,550
But there were still bigger cats out there.

142
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:55,620
Which brings us back to Sparta. The cave lion lived across Europe and northern Asia.

143
00:06:55,670 --> 00:06:58,090
It was larger than modern African lions.

144
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:01,200
Males may have weighed well over two hundred kilograms,

145
00:07:01,250 --> 00:07:04,480
and some estimates go higher. We know what these lions looked like

146
00:07:04,530 --> 00:07:07,050
better than almost any other Ice Age predator,

147
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:09,220
because early humans painted them.

148
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:11,590
In Chauvet Cave in France, more than thirty

149
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:15,050
thousand years ago, people drew lions on the walls.

150
00:07:15,100 --> 00:07:17,820
They drew them hunting. They drew them in groups.

151
00:07:17,870 --> 00:07:20,470
And something about those drawings stands out.

152
00:07:20,520 --> 00:07:24,870
The male lions don't seem to have the large manes of modern African lions.

153
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:26,810
That suggests something important.

154
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:29,510
These people weren't drawing from imagination.

155
00:07:29,560 --> 00:07:31,940
They were drawing animals they had seen.

156
00:07:31,990 --> 00:07:35,107
Maybe too closely. Could you beat a cave lion?

157
00:07:35,157 --> 00:07:38,920
No. A modern African lion can bring down a buffalo.

158
00:07:38,970 --> 00:07:40,970
A bigger lion adapted to the cold,

159
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:43,380
hunting reindeer, horses and bison,

160
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:46,500
would make a fight with an unarmed human very short.

161
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:49,250
And yet, there's still one more animal.

162
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:53,070
One that, when it stood up, might have looked down on all of them.

163
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,690
The short-faced bear lived in North America.

164
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:58,650
For decades, it had a terrifying reputation.

165
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:01,250
With its long legs and short, wide face,

166
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,810
scientists once imagined it as a super-predator.

167
00:08:03,860 --> 00:08:07,900
A running bear that chased down prey across open plains.

168
00:08:07,950 --> 00:08:10,720
Large individuals may have weighed close to a ton.

169
00:08:10,770 --> 00:08:14,790
Standing on its hind legs, it may have reached more than three meters tall.

170
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:17,120
But then researchers looked more closely.

171
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:21,150
Chemical studies of its bones, along with analyses of its skull and teeth,

172
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,270
started to challenge the old picture.

173
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:26,590
Some scientists now think the short-faced bear may have eaten a mix of meat

174
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:28,850
and plants, like many bears today.

175
00:08:28,900 --> 00:08:31,110
Others suggest it may have scavenged,

176
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:34,700
using its sheer size to drive other predators off their kills.

177
00:08:34,750 --> 00:08:38,510
The debate continues. But for you, the details don't matter.

178
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:40,950
An animal that may have weighed close to a ton,

179
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:42,890
with claws and teeth to match,

180
00:08:42,940 --> 00:08:45,430
doesn't need to be a specialist hunter to win.

181
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:48,785
Even the predators that killed mammoths might have backed away from it.

182
00:08:48,835 --> 00:08:52,350
So, which Ice Age predator could you actually beat?

183
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,610
The honest answer is none of them.

184
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:56,570
Not the wolves. Not the hyenas.

185
00:08:56,620 --> 00:08:59,340
Not the cats. And definitely not the bear.

186
00:08:59,390 --> 00:09:01,290
But that's not the end of the story.

187
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:03,890
Because humans didn't just survive in this world.

188
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:06,450
We stayed. Early humans didn't fight these

189
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:09,280
animals with their bare hands. They used spears.

190
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:12,810
They used fire. They planned, they cooperated and they passed

191
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:15,580
knowledge down from one generation to the next.

192
00:09:15,630 --> 00:09:18,910
And something strange happened over the next thousands of years.

193
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:22,400
The dire wolf disappeared. Smilodon disappeared.

194
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:25,100
Homotherium, the cave lion, the cave hyena

195
00:09:25,150 --> 00:09:27,970
and the short-faced bear all disappeared.

196
00:09:28,020 --> 00:09:30,710
Why? Scientists still argue about it.

197
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,820
Climate change at the end of the Ice Age clearly played a role.

198
00:09:33,870 --> 00:09:36,210
But many researchers think humans did too,

199
00:09:36,260 --> 00:09:39,250
by hunting the same prey these predators depended on,

200
00:09:39,300 --> 00:09:42,180
and sometimes by hunting the predators themselves.

201
00:09:42,230 --> 00:09:44,220
In the end, the weakest animal in this

202
00:09:44,270 --> 00:09:47,320
whole story was the only one still standing.

203
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:49,180
Sparta, the frozen cub,

204
00:09:49,230 --> 00:09:51,730
was buried long before any of this happened.

205
00:09:51,780 --> 00:09:54,670
It never got to see what its world became.
